From a97503572ae332ae9717cfe581d4a0b29bda4794 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ayush Das <das.ayush4890@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 11:51:31 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] feat: add /api/health, plus Dockerfile.patch for offline
 rebuilds
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

/api/health did not exist. The CV-Sync KB documented it as available, and a
container healthcheck pointed at it — which 404s, so the service would be
marked permanently unhealthy and anything with `depends_on: service_healthy`
would refuse to start. Registered before the routers so it cannot be shadowed
by /api/:something.

Dockerfile.patch rebuilds only the application code on top of an
already-built image. The normal Dockerfile starts FROM
minidocks/texlive:2023-medium, which cannot be pulled where a TLS-inspecting
proxy mangles docker.io responses. Since only src/ changes between builds,
reusing the existing image skips the registry and takes seconds instead of a
full TeXLive install:

    docker build -f Dockerfile.patch -t cvsync-cv-builder:latest .

Verified: /api/health returns {"ok":true,"service":"CV_BUILDER"}, the container
reports healthy, and a test compile returns X-Page-Count: 1.
---
 Dockerfile.patch | 28 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 src/index.js     |  7 +++++++
 2 files changed, 35 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 Dockerfile.patch

diff --git a/Dockerfile.patch b/Dockerfile.patch
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5425e71
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Dockerfile.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@
+# Rebuild CV_BUILDER's application code on top of an ALREADY-BUILT image.
+#
+# The normal Dockerfile starts FROM minidocks/texlive:2023-medium, which cannot
+# be pulled on a network where a TLS-inspecting proxy mangles docker.io
+# responses (every tag reports "not found" or a 502). Since only src/ changes,
+# reusing the existing image as the base skips the registry entirely and takes
+# seconds instead of a full TeXLive install.
+#
+# Usage (from cv_builder_src/):
+#   docker build -f Dockerfile.patch -t cvsync-cv-builder:latest .
+#
+# BASE defaults to the previously built compose image; override if yours is
+# named differently:
+#   docker build -f Dockerfile.patch --build-arg BASE=cv-builder:local -t cvsync-cv-builder:latest .
+ARG BASE=cvsync-cv-builder:latest
+FROM ${BASE}
+
+WORKDIR /app
+
+# Only the application code changes; TeXLive, tlmgr packages, fonts and
+# node_modules all come from the base image.
+COPY src ./src
+COPY package*.json ./
+
+EXPOSE 3000
+ENV PORT=3000
+
+CMD ["node", "src/index.js"]
diff --git a/src/index.js b/src/index.js
index 3d1a7ab..1a19e0b 100644
--- a/src/index.js
+++ b/src/index.js
@@ -10,6 +10,13 @@ const app = express();
 app.use(cors());
 app.use(express.json({ limit: '5mb' }));
 
+// Liveness probe. Documented in the CV-Sync KB as already existing, but it
+// never did — /api/health returned 404, so any container healthcheck pointed
+// at it would mark this service permanently unhealthy and block anything
+// depending on it from starting. Registered before the routers so it can never
+// be shadowed by /api/:something.
+app.get('/api/health', (req, res) => res.json({ ok: true, service: 'CV_BUILDER' }));
+
 app.use('/api', latexRouter);
 app.use('/api/templates', templateRouter);
 
